# Isolated vector diagrams

A diagram renderer is an optional asset producer, not the book's page-layout
engine. Ordinary Mermaid fences become vector PDF and SVG companions. LaTeX
fits the PDF using the same measured bounds as local illustrations; the SVG
preserves editable vector content. No browser is needed for books without diagrams.

The author explicitly selects an installed Chromium executable. Puppeteer Core
has no browser downloader. Its Node requirement belongs to this CLI feature,
not to the extension's runtime. A separate process keeps its ESM dependencies
and browser lifecycle outside normal generation and editor activation.


## Bounded requests

Only the source text and a catalogue theme cross the worker boundary. Source
locations remain in the controller, where failures can identify the chapter.
The adapter contract returns bytes, never destinations or writes to the project.

```ts : <<Mermaid controller imports>>=
import * as fs from 'fs/promises';
import { constants } from 'fs';
import * as path from 'path';
import { tmpdir } from 'os';
import { spawn } from 'child_process';
import type { PublicationAsset } from './publication-pandoc';
import { getTheme } from './themes';
export interface DiagramBytes { pdf: Uint8Array; svg: Uint8Array }
export type DiagramRenderer = (assets: readonly PublicationAsset[], theme: string,
    executable?: string, checkCancelled?: () => void) => Promise<Map<string, DiagramBytes>>;
export const diagramLimits = { count: 64, source: 50000, pdf: 16 * 1024 * 1024,
    svg: 4 * 1024 * 1024, total: 32 * 1024 * 1024, timeMs: 120000 };
export type DiagramWorker = (directory: string, executable: string) => Promise<void>;
```

Mermaid configuration is renderer-owned. Directives, frontmatter, authored HTML,
CSS declarations and interaction commands are rejected, rather than relying on
Mermaid to ignore them. The browser additionally validates the resulting SVG.
The policy restricts capabilities, not diagram families: Mermaid's own detector
selects any registered renderer. Quoted labels and comments do not act as commands;
the keyword check masks them while preserving separators. Unquoted labels containing
reserved command words should be quoted. HTML checks still examine the original
source, including entity-encoded openers, regardless of quoting.

```ts : <<Mermaid source policy>>=
export function validateDiagram(source: string): void {
    if (!source.trim() || source.length > diagramLimits.source) {
        throw new Error('Mermaid source must contain 1–50000 characters');
    }
    if (/%%\s*\{/.test(source) || /^\s*---(?:\s|$)/.test(source)) {
        throw new Error('Mermaid directives and frontmatter are disabled');
    }
    if (/\b(?:style|classDef|linkStyle|click|links?)\s/i.test(commandText(source))
        || /<\s*\/?\s*[a-z][^>]*>/i.test(source)
        || /&(?:lt|#0*60|#x0*3c);/i.test(source)) {
        throw new Error('Mermaid authored HTML, CSS and interactions are disabled');
    }
}
```

Only double-quoted strings and line comments are masked. Escaped characters stay
inside their string; a newline resets an unterminated quote so it cannot hide
commands on subsequent lines. This small lexical pass does not replace Mermaid's
parser or grant any additional browser capability.

```ts : <<Mermaid source policy>>=+
function commandText(source: string): string {
    let result = '', quoted = false, comment = false;
    for (let index = 0; index < source.length; index++) {
        const character = source[index];
        if (character === '\n' || character === '\r') {
            quoted = false; comment = false; result += character;
        } else if (comment) { result += ' '; }
        else if (quoted && character === '\\') {
            result += ' ';
            if (index + 1 < source.length && !/[\r\n]/.test(source[index + 1])) { index++; result += ' '; }
        } else if (character === '"') { quoted = !quoted; result += ' '; }
        else if (!quoted && character === '%' && source[index + 1] === '%') {
            comment = true; result += ' ';
        } else { result += quoted ? ' ' : character; }
    }
    return result;
}
```

## Process lifetime

A POSIX process group lets the parent terminate the worker and Chromium together,
including when browser close hangs. Normal completion also kills any descendants
left by a failed close. Neither the shell nor a project-supplied configuration is
used. The deadline and log cap remain enforceable even when browser JavaScript
is stuck. Windows is rejected explicitly until equivalent process-tree ownership
is available.

```ts : <<Mermaid worker execution>>=
export function executeDiagramWorker(directory: string, executable: string,
    worker = path.join(__dirname, 'publication-mermaid-worker.mjs'),
    timeoutMs = diagramLimits.timeMs): Promise<void> {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const child = spawn(process.execPath, ['--max-old-space-size=256', worker, directory, executable],
            { detached: true, stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'], windowsHide: true });
        let failure = '', log = '', size = 0;
        const kill = (): void => {
            if (!child.pid) { return; }
            try { process.kill(-child.pid, 'SIGKILL'); }
            catch (error) { if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== 'ESRCH') { failure ||= String(error); } }
        };
        <<Mermaid worker events>>
    });
}
```

The child reports bounded diagnostics only; binary results remain in the private
directory. An exit status alone is insufficient: all expected artifacts are
validated after the child exits. Any error prevents the publication transaction.

```ts : <<Mermaid worker events>>=
const timer = setTimeout(() => { failure = 'Mermaid browser deadline exceeded'; kill(); }, timeoutMs);
const interrupt = (): void => { failure = 'Mermaid rendering interrupted'; kill(); };
const cleanup = (): void => {
    clearTimeout(timer); process.removeListener('SIGINT', interrupt); process.removeListener('SIGTERM', interrupt);
};
process.on('SIGINT', interrupt); process.on('SIGTERM', interrupt);
for (const stream of [child.stdout, child.stderr]) {
    stream.on('data', (data: Buffer) => {
        size += data.length;
        if (size > 65536) { failure = 'Mermaid worker log exceeds 64 KiB'; kill(); }
        else { log += data.toString('utf8'); }
    });
}
child.once('error', error => { cleanup(); kill(); reject(error); });
child.once('close', (code, signal) => {
    cleanup(); kill();
    if (failure || code !== 0) {
        reject(new Error(`${failure || `Mermaid worker exited ${code ?? signal}`}: ${log.trim()}`));
    } else { resolve(); }
});
```

Regular-file and byte limits are checked before allocation and again afterwards.
Worker filenames are numeric indexes chosen by the controller, never source paths.
Chromium PDF timestamps are normalized in the worker, allowing unchanged exports
with the same browser, Mermaid version and installed diagram fonts.

```ts : <<Mermaid artifact reads>>=
async function diagramArtifact(directory: string, index: number, format: 'pdf' | 'svg',
    checkCancelled?: () => void): Promise<Uint8Array> {
    const file = path.join(directory, `${index}.${format}`);
    const stat = await fs.lstat(file);
    checkCancelled?.();
    if (!stat.isFile() || stat.size < 1 || stat.size > diagramLimits[format]) {
        throw new Error(`Invalid or oversized Mermaid ${format} artifact`);
    }
    const bytes = await fs.readFile(file);
    checkCancelled?.();
    if (bytes.length > diagramLimits[format] || (format === 'pdf'
        ? bytes.subarray(0, 5).toString() !== '%PDF-' : !bytes.toString('utf8').startsWith('<svg'))) {
        throw new Error(`Invalid Mermaid ${format} artifact`);
    }
    return bytes;
}
```

## Explicit browser selection

The executable must be an absolute path to a trusted, executable regular file.
Symlinks at this explicit system-tool boundary are allowed, unlike project image
paths. There is no PATH discovery, browser cache lookup, installation or sandbox
fallback. Identical source strings render once for the selected theme.

```ts : <<Mermaid rendering request>>=
export async function renderDiagrams(assets: readonly PublicationAsset[], theme: string,
    executable?: string, worker: DiagramWorker = executeDiagramWorker,
    checkCancelled?: () => void): Promise<Map<string, DiagramBytes>> {
    checkCancelled?.();
    const sources = [...new Set(assets.map(asset => asset.source))];
    if (!sources.length) { return new Map(); }
    for (const asset of assets) {
        try { validateDiagram(asset.source); }
        catch (error) { throw new Error(`${asset.at.path}:${asset.at.line + 1}: ${String(error)}`); }
    }
    if (sources.length > diagramLimits.count) { throw new Error('Publication exceeds 64 unique Mermaid diagrams'); }
    if (!getTheme(theme)) { throw new Error('Unknown Mermaid publication theme'); }
    if (!executable || !path.isAbsolute(executable) || /[\u0000-\u001f]/.test(executable)) {
        throw new Error('Mermaid requires --browser /absolute/path/to/chromium; install a system browser explicitly. No browser is downloaded.');
    }
    <<Mermaid runtime checks>>
    <<Mermaid temporary rendering>>
}
```

Puppeteer Core requires Node 22.12 or newer. This check runs only for diagrams,
so it neither imposes a new extension engine requirement nor launches a browser
during normal generation. Failure preserves the successful publication.

```ts : <<Mermaid runtime checks>>=
const [major, minor] = process.versions.node.split('.').map(Number);
if (major < 22 || (major === 22 && minor < 12)) { throw new Error('Mermaid export requires Node >=22.12'); }
if (process.platform === 'win32') { throw new Error('Mermaid export requires POSIX process-group cleanup'); }
try {
    const stat = await fs.stat(executable);
    checkCancelled?.();
    if (!stat.isFile()) { throw new Error('Not a regular file'); }
    await fs.access(executable, constants.X_OK);
} catch (error) {
    checkCancelled?.();
    throw new Error(`Cannot execute --browser ${executable}: ${String(error)}`);
}
checkCancelled?.();
```

The private directory owns both profile and artifacts, and is removed on every
completion path. No writes reach the project until the caller receives a complete
result. Cleanup errors are surfaced rather than reporting a successful export
while leaving an undisclosed browser profile behind.

The caller supplies a persistent cancellation check, including cancellation latched
before a lazy renderer import. Checks bracket asynchronous preparation and artifact
reads. The final preparation check immediately precedes synchronous worker spawn;
worker signal handlers own interruption while the child runs. Once a temporary
directory exists, cancellation always passes through its cleanup scope.

```ts : <<Mermaid temporary rendering>>=
const directory = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(tmpdir(), 'literate-mermaid-'));
try {
    checkCancelled?.();
    await fs.writeFile(path.join(directory, 'request.json'), JSON.stringify({ sources, theme }), { mode: 0o600 });
    checkCancelled?.();
    try { await worker(directory, executable); }
    catch (error) {
        const index = Number(/Diagram (\d+):/.exec(String(error))?.[1] ?? 1) - 1;
        const asset = assets.find(asset => asset.source === sources[index]) ?? assets[0];
        throw new Error(`${asset.at.path}:${asset.at.line + 1}: ${String(error)}`);
    }
    checkCancelled?.();
    const result = new Map<string, DiagramBytes>();
    let total = 0;
    for (const [index, source] of sources.entries()) {
        const pdf = await diagramArtifact(directory, index, 'pdf', checkCancelled);
        checkCancelled?.();
        const svg = await diagramArtifact(directory, index, 'svg', checkCancelled);
        checkCancelled?.();
        total += pdf.length + svg.length;
        if (total > diagramLimits.total) { throw new Error('Mermaid artifacts exceed 32 MiB'); }
        result.set(source, { pdf, svg });
    }
    return result;
} finally { await fs.rm(directory, { recursive: true, force: true, maxRetries: 3, retryDelay: 50 }); }
```
